<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#gauleiter">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gauleiter</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A Gauleiter was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a>'s leader of a regional branch, or Gau, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and later in annexed territories. Appointed by Hitler and answerable to him alone, Gauleiters often also held state offices such as Reich governor, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, as Reich defence commissioners, they gained wide powers over civil defence, labour and evacuation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gauleiter.jpg" alt="Gauleiter" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tractor · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gauleiter.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> first used the term Gauleiter in 1925, around the time <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> re-founded the Party on 27 February, after the lifting of the ban imposed in the aftermath of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beer-hall-putsch">Beer Hall Putsch</a> of 9 November 1923. The word comes from Gau, an old term for a region of the German Reich, and leiter, leader. In the early years local activists organised the Party in their home areas and were sometimes chosen by the members themselves, which Hitler officially prohibited only in 1929. The Gaue did not necessarily follow existing administrative divisions. There were 35 in 1928 and 43 by the end of the regime.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Gauleiters were appointed by Hitler, were personally responsible to him and could be dismissed at once. Gauleiter was the third-highest rank in the Nazi political leadership, below Reichsleiter and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuhrer">Führer</a>. A Gauleiter headed the Gauleitung and had authority over the Kreisleiters, who directed the Ortsgruppenleiters, Zellenleiters and Blockleiters, but he had no direct control over the SA or SS. About 125 men held the position. Many were old fighters of the Kampfzeit, born in the last decade of the 19th century, who had served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and often in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">Freikorps</a>; later appointees were younger and trained as administrators.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>After the Nazis took power on 30 January 1933, Gauleiters became the key agents in extending Nazi control over the German states. The Second Law on the Coordination of the States with the Reich of 7 April 1933 created the post of Reichsstatthalter (Reich Governor), which Hitler filled almost exclusively with Gauleiters. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anschluss">Anschluss</a> of 12 March 1938 and the seizure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudetenland">Sudetenland</a>, the annexed territories were organised into eight Reichsgaue under Reichsstatthalters who were also the local Gauleiters. Gauleiters carried out the Party's persecution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, and on 9 and 10 November 1938 they ordered riots in their Gaue after an address by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-goebbels">Joseph Goebbels</a>.</p><p>On 1 September 1939 fifteen Gauleiters were appointed Reich Defense Commissioners, charged with civil defence, mobilisation, evacuation and rationing. On 16 November 1942 all 42 territorial Gauleiters became commissioners in their own Gaue, registering men and women between sixteen and sixty for war work and overseeing defensive fortifications built with forced labour. In October 1944 each organised the Volkssturm. Under the Nero Decree of 19 March 1945 many refused to destroy infrastructure, but several ordered bridges, plants and factories destroyed. Of the 43 serving in May 1945, twelve killed themselves, eight were executed as war criminals and sixteen were imprisoned.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gauleiter” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Nazi regime annexed it in all but name under the <b>Gauleiter</b> Gustav Simon, imposed German law and in 1942 introduced conscription of young Luxembourgers into the Wehrmacht, which provoked a general strike that was brutally repressed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In practice government was a tangle of overlapping agencies, which historians call polycracy: old ministries coexisted with party offices, the <b>Gauleiter</b> who ran regional party districts, and special plenipotentiaries such as Göring for the economy and, from 1942, Albert Speer for armaments.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
